---
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site, url } from '../../data/site';
import { LEVELS, describe, type Axis } from '../../lib/aisignoff';

const tool = 'https://github.com/industrial-mathematics-group/aisignoff';
const docs = 'https://industrial-mathematics-group.github.io/aisignoff/';
const pypi = 'https://pypi.org/project/aisignoff/';
const doi = '10.5281/zenodo.24020702';
const version = '1.0.0rc2';
// The deploy workflow writes this site's own folder report into public/signoff/ before the build.
const ownReport = existsSync('public/signoff/index.html');
const axes: { key: Axis; letter: string; title: string; question: string; slug: string }[] = [
  { key: 'generation', letter: 'G', title: 'Generation', question: 'How much of it did an AI generate?', slug: 'generation' },
  { key: 'human', letter: 'H', title: 'Human review', question: 'How thoroughly did a human review it?', slug: 'human-review' },
  { key: 'ai', letter: 'A', title: 'AI review', question: 'How thoroughly did an AI review it?', slug: 'ai-review' },
];
const initial = { generation: 2, human: 2, ai: 2 };
const example = describe(initial);
// The example of aisignoff's documentation (Declaring lines): how the lines of one file change.
const solver = [
  ['A pull request adds it, declared G0 H2 A0', '200 lines G0 H2 A0'],
  ['Another rewrites 100 lines, declared G2 H1 A1', '100 lines G0 H2 A0, 100 lines G2 H1 A1'],
  ['A person reviews the file line by line and attests it', '100 lines G0 H2 A0, 100 lines G2 H2 A1'],
  ['A third changes 10 lines, declared G2 H1 A1', 'those 10 lines are H1 again, the other 190 stay H2'],
];
---

<Layout title="AI declarations" description="How we declare the use of AI in our code, file by file and line by line, and aisignoff, the tool that checks it.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>AI declarations</h1>
      <p>We use AI tools in our work, and every contribution says how it was created and how it was checked. A declaration such as <strong>G2 · H2 · A2</strong> reads: generated by AI, reviewed line by line by a human, and reviewed by a new AI session. Our tool <a href={tool}>aisignoff</a> keeps these declarations for every file, and every line, of a repository.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Three scales</h2>
      <div class="scales">
        {axes.map((axis) => (
          <div class={`scale scale-${axis.slug}`}>
            <h3>{axis.letter}: {axis.title}</h3>
            <p class="scale-question">{axis.question}</p>
            <ol>
              {LEVELS[axis.key].map((l) => (
                <li><strong>{l.level} {l.name}.</strong> {l.meaning}</li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          </div>
        ))}
        <div class="prose scale-rules">
          <p>Two rules connect the scales. AI-written content must at least be checked by the session that wrote it, so G1 to G3 need A1 or higher. Human-written content has no such session, so G0 is never A1: an AI review of it counts as A2 or A3. A human is always responsible, whatever wrote the code, and reviews at A2 or A3 are committed to the repository.</p>
          <p><a href={site.aiPolicy}>The full AI policy</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build a declaration</h2>
      <div>
        <form class="builder" data-builder>
          {axes.map((axis) => (
            <fieldset class={`scale-${axis.slug}`}>
              <legend>{axis.title}</legend>
              {LEVELS[axis.key].map((l, i) => (
                <label title={l.meaning}>
                  <input type="radio" name={axis.key} value={i} checked={initial[axis.key] === i} />
                  <span>{l.level}</span> {l.name}
                </label>
              ))}
            </fieldset>
          ))}
        </form>
        <div class="builder-result" aria-live="polite">
          <p class="builder-label" data-builder-label>{example.label}</p>
          <p data-builder-sentence>{example.sentence}</p>
          <ul class="builder-problems" data-builder-problems hidden></ul>
          <p class="builder-caption">Review gap (generation level minus human review level): <strong data-builder-gap>{example.gap}</strong></p>
          <p class="builder-caption">In a pull request description or a commit message, for the lines it changes:</p>
          <pre><code data-builder-lines>{example.lines}</code></pre>
          <p class="builder-caption">In <code>SIGNOFF.toml</code>, for the files in <code>src/</code>:</p>
          <pre><code data-builder-toml>{example.toml}</code></pre>
          <p class="builder-caption">Or written by the tool:</p>
          <pre><code data-builder-command>{example.command}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Files and lines</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>Files.</strong> A <code>SIGNOFF.toml</code> maps paths to levels, like <a href="https://reuse.software">REUSE</a> does for licences: a default for every file, then exceptions for folders and globs, where the last matching entry wins. A file can also carry its levels in a comment in its first lines. This needs nothing but the file, and suits results, data, notebooks and papers.</p>
        <p><strong>Lines.</strong> With <code>[history]</code> in <code>SIGNOFF.toml</code>, each line is declared by the commit that landed it on the default branch. For a pull request that is its merge commit, whose message is the pull request's description with the ticked declaration. A pull request that changes 35 lines of an 800-line file changes the levels of those 35 lines. Every line has one owner, and lines whose commit declares nothing keep the declaration of their file.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>A 200-line file</th><th>Its lines</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {solver.map(([step, result]) => <tr><td>{step}</td><td>{result}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Generation follows the line and changes only when the line changes. A review can be raised later for a whole file with <code>aisignoff attest</code>, which records the content that was checked, so it never covers code written after the review. A snapshot (<code>aisignoff freeze</code>) records the levels of every line, so that a new release or a changed annotation can't change the levels of code that landed long ago.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Reports</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><code>aisignoff report</code> sums up a repository per axis, and <code>report --tree</code> shows the level mix of each folder. <code>report --html</code> writes an interactive folder report, like a coverage report: a sunburst of the folders, a table to sort them, and each file line by line, coloured by generation, human review, AI review or the review gap. It is a single file that needs no server. For a pull request, <code>aisignoff diff</code> shows the levels of just the lines it changes and how the repository's shares move.</p>
        {ownReport && <p>Below is the report of this website, written by aisignoff at its last deployment from the repository's history. Choose a folder in the sunburst or the table to go into it, and a file to read it line by line with the declaration of each line.</p>}
        <ul class="links">
          {ownReport && <li><a href={url('/signoff/')}>The report of this website on its own page</a></li>}
          <li><a href={`${docs}signoff/`}>The report of aisignoff</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    {ownReport && (
      <iframe class="signoff-report" src={url('/signoff/')} title="The aisignoff report of this website" loading="lazy"></iframe>
    )}

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The tool: aisignoff</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><a href={tool}>aisignoff</a> checks the declarations in CI, the way a test suite checks code. The name is meant both ways: the AI signs off, and so does the human. It needs Python 3.11 or newer and git, and installs from <a href={pypi}>PyPI</a>:</p>
        <pre><code># or: uv tool install aisignoff, or: pip install aisignoff
pipx install aisignoff

# a default for every file
aisignoff init -g G0 -H H2 -a A0 --reviewed-by "Jane Doe"

# the exceptions, by file, folder or glob
aisignoff annotate results/ -g G2 -H H1 -a A1 \
    --assisted-by "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)" \
    --ai-reviewed-by "Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), self-check by the generating session" \
    --reviewed-by "Jane Doe"

# is every file, and with [history] every line, declared?
aisignoff lint</code></pre>
        <p><code>annotate</code> refuses declarations that break the rules above, and <code>lint</code> fails while anything lacks a valid declaration or falls below the repository's minimum levels, set in a <code>[policy]</code> table, for example a line-by-line human review for everything an agent wrote. Any level can be left as <code>TODO</code> for the person who will review it; <code>lint</code> reports it until it is filled in. Further commands write the reports and badges, attest reviews and take snapshots.</p>
        <p>Our repositories use the organisation's ready-made workflows: <em>File-signoff</em> runs <code>aisignoff lint</code> on every push and pull request, writes the folder report and publishes the badges; <em>PR-signoff</em> checks the declaration ticked in each pull request, and <em>Signoff-Badges</em> sums up the merged ones. A pre-commit hook runs the same check before each commit.</p>
        <p>The current release is {version}, a release candidate for 1.0, from which the formats and commands follow semantic versioning. Up to version 0.3.0 the tool was called aidecl; release 1.0.0rc1 converts an old <code>AIDECL.toml</code> once, since later releases no longer read it.</p>
        <ul class="links">
          <li><a href={docs}>Documentation</a></li>
          <li><a href={tool}>Code</a></li>
          <li><a href={pypi}>PyPI</a></li>
          <li><a href={`https://doi.org/${doi}`}>DOI</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import { describe } from '../../lib/aisignoff';

  const form = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-builder]');
  const field = (name: string) => document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-builder-${name}]`)!;
  if (form) {
    const update = () => {
      const data = new FormData(form);
      const d = describe({ generation: Number(data.get('generation')), human: Number(data.get('human')), ai: Number(data.get('ai')) });
      field('label').textContent = d.label;
      field('sentence').textContent = d.sentence;
      field('gap').textContent = String(d.gap);
      field('lines').textContent = d.lines;
      field('toml').textContent = d.toml;
      field('command').textContent = d.command;
      const list = field('problems');
      list.replaceChildren(...d.problems.map((text) => Object.assign(document.createElement('li'), { textContent: text })));
      list.hidden = d.problems.length === 0;
      field('label').classList.toggle('is-invalid', d.problems.length > 0);
    };
    form.addEventListener('change', update);
    update();
  }
</script>
